\subsection{From scores to probabilities}
A model gives each candidate a score. The softmax turns scores into probabilities that add up to one.

\begin{definition}[temperature scaling]
Dividing every score by a positive number $T$ before the softmax is called \term{temperature scaling}{溫度縮放}.
\end{definition}

\begin{intuition}
A small $T$ makes the largest score win almost everything; a large $T$ spreads the probability out.
\end{intuition}

So the temperature sets how sharp the distribution is.\sidenote{溫度越小，分布越尖。}

The number $T$ is the \term{softmax temperature}{softmax 溫度}. It changes no ranking, only how sure the probabilities look.

\begin{keyidea}
Temperature trades sharpness against smoothness.
\end{keyidea}

\subsection{Pulling pairs together}
A \term{contrastive loss}{對比損失} rewards a model when two views of one example score higher than views of other examples.

\begin{definition}[hard negative]
A \term{hard negative}{困難負樣本} is another example whose score is close to the positive one.
\end{definition}

Hard negatives carry most of the learning signal, because easy ones already get almost no probability once a small temperature is used.\sidenote{困難負樣本提供大部分的學習訊號。}

\begin{remark}
These notes are synthetic: they exist to try the flow without a model.
\end{remark}
